{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent Crusader nobleman and ruler who lived during the late 12th and early 13th centuries. He was born around 1140 as the eldest son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His family was one of the most powerful in the Latin Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch holding significant territories in modern-day Lebanon Syria and Israel.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early Life and Military Career**\nRaymond III grew up in a politically turbulent region where conflicts between Crusader states and Muslim powers were frequent. His father Raymond II was an influential figure in the Crusader world contributing to the defense of Antioch against Zengi and Nur ad-Din. Young Raymond was educated in the military arts and chivalry preparing him for leadership. By the time he reached adulthood he had already participated in several military campaigns including the siege of Harenc in 1164 where he likely fought alongside his father. His early experiences shaped him into a capable warrior and statesman.\n\nInheriting the County of Tripoli upon his father’s death in 1152 Raymond III became one of the most powerful Crusader lords. His domain included the city of Tripoli a key port on the Mediterranean coast and extensive lands in the surrounding region. As count he was responsible for defending his territories from threats posed by the Ayyubid dynasty which was expanding under the leadership of Saladin. Raymond III maintained diplomatic relations with both Christian and Muslim rulers attempting to secure alliances and avoid direct conflicts whenever possible.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Alliance with Saladin and the Kingdom of Jerusalem**\nOne of Raymond III’s most significant diplomatic moves was his alliance with Saladin the famous Muslim leader who unified much of the Middle East under his rule. This alliance was initially formed in 1176 when Raymond III sought to balance the power of the Kingdom of Jerusalem led by Baldwin IV. The young king Baldwin IV suffered from leprosy and was often unable to lead military campaigns effectively. Raymond III and Saladin agreed to a truce that allowed Raymond to keep his territories while Saladin focused on other regions but this alliance later strained due to shifting political dynamics in the Crusader states.\n\nRaymond III’s relationship with Baldwin IV was complex marked by both cooperation and tension. When Baldwin IV launched a campaign against Mosul in 1176 Raymond III supported him despite personal reservations. However he also engaged in secret negotiations with Saladin aiming to protect his own interests. After Baldwin IV’s death in 1185 Raymond III played a role in the succession crisis that followed with his support for Baldwin V the young heir to the throne. However Baldwin V died suddenly in 1186 leaving the kingdom vulnerable.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**The Siege of Tripoli and Later Life**\nRaymond III’s greatest challenge came in 1187 when Saladin launched a full-scale invasion of the Crusader states following the Battle of Hattin. The defeat at Hattin was catastrophic for the Crusaders resulting in the capture of Jerusalem and the near-collapse of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond III initially resisted Saladin’s advances holding out in Tripoli but he eventually surrendered the city in September 1187 after a brief siege. As part of the surrender agreement Raymond III was allowed to retain his title and some of his lands on the condition that he paid tribute to Saladin.\n\nAfter the surrender Raymond III lived out his remaining years in a reduced capacity. He continued to play a role in the political struggles of the Crusader states although his influence waned following the loss of Tripoli. He died around 1197 his legacy marked by his ability to navigate the complex alliances and conflicts of his time. Despite his eventual submission to Saladin Raymond III was respected as a skilled diplomat and military leader who did his best to preserve the Crusader presence in the region against overwhelming odds. His story reflects the broader challenges faced by the Latin states in the Levant during the Crusades.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00012003655137959868}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.006313735153526068}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.29494979977607727}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00038413776201196015}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.01811952330172062}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.4808386564254761}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.10021013766527176}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -1.2508610486984253}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.5450923442840576}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -8.546940807718784e-05}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.7644969820976257}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.11764898151159286}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.6093230843544006}, {"token": " ruler", "logprob": -0.9852011203765869}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.6353282332420349}, {"token": " lived", "logprob": -0.9770353436470032}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.06228756904602051}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.04611261561512947}, {"token": " late", "logprob": -0.8836123943328857}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.026037877425551414}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.5258672647178173e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.11316455155611038}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -1.0132738680113107e-05}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.06397335976362228}, {"token": " early", "logprob": -0.000125281119835563}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00016449528629891574}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.6689160474925302e-05}, {"token": "3", "logprob": -0.00034874555421993136}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -0.00013207517622504383}, {"token": " centuries", "logprob": -0.002206987701356411}, {"token": 